      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-15T12:14:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As that thread shows, it\u0027s not a good idea to do this.\n\nAgain, go back to the root issue here, fix the driver to properly not allow users to abuse it.  Better yet, submit that driver upstream for review and then it would be much easier to verify that this symbol really is needed, or not.",
      "parentUuid": "2057126f_b22816fd",
      "revId": "9c84b168942e270be4126df6f8b3c85c6249048b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d240401f_0933add2",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 3334031
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-15T12:28:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding the UAF issue, we have already removed the logic in the kernel driver that could cause UAF. However, we want to further enhance security by allowing only critical processes, such as watchdog, to access the monitorhang node via ioctl. To achieve this, we plan to perform a security check in the kernel layer by obtaining the Security Identifier (SID) of the process, and only permit specified critical processes to access the monitorhang node. The use of the security_cred_getsecid API is solely for implementing this security access control mechanism, so it is necessary to export this symbol.",
      "parentUuid": "bae3de51_50ca61fb",
      "revId": "9c84b168942e270be4126df6f8b3c85c6249048b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "726fe5a7_aff15b46",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-15T12:30:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just use a normal selinux rule to control access for this if you want, no need to do this type of check at all.  Otherwise you would need to do that for ALL device node accesses, which is not viable at all.",
      "parentUuid": "d240401f_0933add2",
      "revId": "9c84b168942e270be4126df6f8b3c85c6249048b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf12017c_35c00f38",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 3334031
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-15T12:42:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Some hackers may attempt to disable SELinux and then access the monitorhang node to make the monitorhang driver perform unexpected operations. However, even if SELinux is disabled by the user, the SID (Security Identifier) value remains unique and available. By checking the SID value of the process, we can still provide effective protection and ensure that only specified critical processes are allowed to access the monitorhang node.\nMoreover, this security mechanism will not be applied to all kernel driver nodes. The monitorhang driver has the capability to trigger a system-wide abnormal reboot, making it a prime target for hackers. Therefore, we aim to strengthen the access control for the monitorhang driver node, ensuring that only authorized critical processes can access this node and thereby enhancing the overall system security.",
      "parentUuid": "726fe5a7_aff15b46",
      "revId": "9c84b168942e270be4126df6f8b3c85c6249048b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
